{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-139038",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{10n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{8n+1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{10n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{n(8+\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{10\\sqrt[n]{n}}{(8+\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 1}{8+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=\\frac{5}{4}>1,$ the series diverges by the root test.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
